{"items":[{"targetId":"split-000099","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The occurrence level identifies the sharing context of a particular use, while the replicator level identifies the context represented by the sharing node itself.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Defines the two quantities whose difference is the port delta."}]},{"targetId":"split-000100","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"A delta describes a relative displacement between sharing contexts, not how many replicas a replicator produces.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Separates the structural level annotation from replicator arity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000101","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Levels belong to the net's sharing structure, so they are not runtime values associated with source-language variables.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Distinguishes graph annotations from source-term data."}]},{"targetId":"split-000102","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The two auxiliary ports are already the explicit representation of the two demand sites for the shared binding.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why a level delta need not encode consumer multiplicity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000103","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A consumer site receives a relative annotation because its required sharing context is compared with that of the replicator supplying it.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects relative levels and deltas to consumer-site structure."}]},{"targetId":"split-000104","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"When replicators commute, delta adjustments preserve the relative context encoded at each replica after their structural order changes.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the invariant served by delta adjustment."}]},{"targetId":"split-000105","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Its applicability depends on reachability from the root rather than on finding one active pair of principal ports.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Contrasts global garbage collection with local interaction rewriting."}]},{"targetId":"split-000106","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Reachability marking preserves exactly the subgraph that can still contribute to the root's observable result.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the purpose of starting the sweep at the root."}]},{"targetId":"split-000107","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Material unreachable from the root has no remaining path to the represented result, so it is garbage for that net evaluation.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"supports","reason":"Provides the semantic rationale for deletion."}]},{"targetId":"split-000108","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Replacing the surviving endpoint with an eraser preserves a well-formed termination of the connection after its unreachable neighbor disappears.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why deletion can require an eraser insertion."}]},{"targetId":"split-000109","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Here lA and lB are the replicator levels, and d bounds how far B's level may lie above A's level.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the inequality's variables and interval role explicit."}]},{"targetId":"split-000110","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"The bounded level difference excludes the structural configuration that would make B a matching interaction partner at that stage.","densityScore":0.75,"relation":"supports","reason":"Links the inequality to the unpaired conclusion."}]},{"targetId":"split-000111","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"Because B remains unavailable for interaction, eliminating A first cannot skip an interaction that B was already entitled to perform.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects unpairedness to the ordering constraint."}]},{"targetId":"split-000112","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The inequality establishes that the scheduled reduction order preserves a required structural invariant; it is not merely a strategy for reducing work sooner.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the proof obligation enforced by the condition."}]},{"targetId":"split-000113","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"An auxiliary port ending in an eraser represents a replica whose result is unused, so ΔK removes that dead branch.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the operational meaning of the local cleanup rule."}]},{"targetId":"split-000114","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"With one remaining consumer and zero relative displacement, the replicator no longer represents sharing or a context transition.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why the node can be replaced by direct connectivity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000115","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Local confluence means that competing one-step core interactions can be joined by further reductions whenever both are applicable.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Defines the property attributed to the core rules."}]},{"targetId":"split-000116","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Canonicalization may inspect or rewrite auxiliary-port structure, so its redexes are not restricted to active pairs.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Identifies why canonicalization differs from core interaction."}]},{"targetId":"split-000117","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Without a schedule, overlapping non-principal cleanup or sharing rewrites can leave the whole reduction process underspecified even if core interactions are locally confluent.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Derives the need for a canonicalization strategy from its broader rewrite scope."}]},{"targetId":"split-000118","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An outermost pattern is one not contained inside another reducible pattern; leftmost breaks ties among such patterns according to the net's chosen traversal order.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Defines both components of the scheduling rule."}]},{"targetId":"split-000119","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Scheduling cleanup before later commutations removes dead structure, while scheduling merges earlier consolidates sharing structure before it is moved again.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the stated order among canonicalizations."}]},{"targetId":"split-000120","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The encoding bijection maps the source representation into a net representation satisfying the subsystem's initial canonical-form constraints.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies what it means for φ to create an initial canonical net."}]},{"targetId":"split-000121","injections":[{"type":"transition","text":"Interaction preserves properness while it may temporarily destroy canonical form, which is why canonicalization is performed during reduction rather than only at encoding time.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the transition from canonical to merely proper nets."}]},{"targetId":"split-000122","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Ω denotes the complete operational prescription: which core interactions occur and when the subsystem's canonicalization steps are applied.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Makes the role of Ω explicit."}]},{"targetId":"split-000123","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Readback requires canonical normal form so that the inverse encoding has an unambiguous source-level interpretation.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the precondition on φ−1."}]},{"targetId":"split-000124","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Fan annihilation removes a matching sharing structure when its two sides meet in the interaction pattern defined by ΔL.","densityScore":0.71,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Names the practical effect of ΔL's sole interaction."}]},{"targetId":"split-000125","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Since ΔL has no additional erasure or commuting canonicalizations, properness alone satisfies its canonical-form requirements.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why canonicalization is vacuous in ΔL."}]},{"targetId":"split-000126","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"The claim is a step-for-step simulation result: each source β-reduction corresponds to one net interaction, with no order-dependent extra interactions.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"States the operational significance of the equal reduction counts."}]},{"targetId":"split-000127","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"Unlike ΔL, these systems contain explicit structures for discarded computation and nontrivial sharing that can require normalization outside core interactions.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Contrasts the nonlinear systems with ΔL."}]},{"targetId":"split-000128","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Because cleanup and sharing normalization can act outside active pairs, the algorithm needs an order that coordinates them with ordinary interactions.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why global scheduling is part of the whole-algorithm claim."}]},{"targetId":"split-000129","injections":[{"type":"citation_needed","text":"The precise scope and hypotheses of the claimed Church–Rosser and optimality results should be checked against the paper's formal theorems.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"The strength of these metatheoretic claims depends on their stated conditions."}]},{"targetId":"split-000130","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Formally, if one net reduces along two finite paths to two results, there are further reduction paths from those results to a common net.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Supplies the joinability formulation of Church–Rosser."}]},{"targetId":"split-000131","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Confluence permits intermediate reductions to differ; it constrains eventual joinability rather than requiring identical immediate successors.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Contrasts the property with one-step agreement."}]},{"targetId":"split-000132","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Variable arity allows one replicator to expose the set of consumer sites belonging to a single sharing context without encoding each consumer as a separate binary sharing node.","densityScore":0.81,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the representational purpose of variable arity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000133","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Most computation is expressed by local graph rewrites, while the schedule constrains only the ordering of selected global or canonicalization actions.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Clarifies the division between locality and scheduling discipline."}]},{"targetId":"split-000134","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"By deleting unreachable or unused branches and normalizing sharing before unnecessary propagation, the discipline aims to avoid reductions whose results cannot affect the root.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects scheduling discipline to avoidable-work prevention."}]}]}
